"<span>Blind: hide the model curves, the predicted values and the line names</span>",
"Ionic bond (OpenStax §{#}): taking the outer electron from sodium costs its ionization energy, {#} eV, and giving it to chlorine returns the electron affinity, {#} eV, so the ions Na⁺ and Cl⁻ far apart have ΔE = {#} eV more energy than the neutral atoms. Brought to a separation r they gain the Coulomb energy −ke²/r (point charges). When their electron clouds overlap the exclusion principle pushes them apart. The book describes this repulsion but gives no formula, so the demo uses A/rⁿ with A chosen to put the minimum at the measured separation r₀ = {#} nm; the exponent n sets how stiff the repulsion is and how deep the well is. With n = {#} the depth matches the measured dissociation energy, {#} eV. The balls are drawn in proportion to measured radii (to each other, not to the separation scale) and change size where the ions become the lower energy state. Losing its one 3s electron leaves Na⁺ with only the filled n = {#} shell, held by {#} protons, so it shrinks from about {#} pm (sodium metal) to {#} pm. The extra electron on Cl⁻ is screened from the nucleus by the other {#} and repelled by them, so the ion swells from about {#} pm (half the Cl₂ bond) to {#} pm. The ionic radii (Shannon, {#}) are measured in crystals, where they add to {#} pm, the spacing of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ in rock salt ({#} pm); in a lone NaCl molecule each ion has one neighbor instead of six and the pair sits closer, {#} pm apart. Left out: the polarization of each ion by the other, and the motion of the nuclei (the zero point energy, about {#} eV). Energies are measured from neutral Na and Cl at rest far apart; the neutral atoms are taken not to interact.",
"Covalent bond (OpenStax §{#}): two atoms share a pair of electrons. The curve is a Morse potential U = D_e({#} − e^(−a(r − r_e)))² − D_e, a model with three measured inputs: the bond length r_e, the vibration wavenumber ωe (which fixes a = {#}πcωe√(μ/2D_e)) and the well depth D_e = D₀ + G({#}), where D₀ is the measured dissociation energy and G({#}) the zero point energy of the lowest vibration level (constants from Huber and Herzberg {#} via the NIST Chemistry WebBook). The levels drawn in the well use the measured ωe and ωe xe. The parallel spin curve for H₂ is Sato's anti Morse form, a model of the repulsive (antibonding) state, shown for the idea only. OpenStax quotes {#} nm and {#} eV for H₂; the spectroscopic values are r_e = {#} nm and D₀ = {#} eV (D_e = {#} eV).",
"H₂⁺ orbitals: the hydrogen molecular ion has one electron, so its orbitals can be drawn exactly as a model gives them. The LCAO model (linear combination of atomic orbitals) builds them from the 1s orbital φ of each proton: the bonding orbital σg = (φ_A + φ_B)/√({#}({#} + S)) and the antibonding orbital σu* = (φ_A − φ_B)/√({#}({#} − S)), where S is the overlap of the two atomic orbitals. In σg the two waves add between the protons, so the electron spends more time there, attracted by both; in σu* they cancel on the midplane, a node. The energy from H and a proton far apart is U = ({#}/R − (j ± k)/({#} ± S)) E_h with R in units of a₀ (Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics, §{#}; Atkins, Physical Chemistry, §{#}). The protons are held fixed (Born–Oppenheimer). The model is not exact: its well is {#} eV deep at {#} nm, against the measured {#} eV at {#} nm, because the real orbital also shrinks toward the protons. The measured curve is a Morse potential through the measured r_e, ωe and D₀ (Huber and Herzberg {#} via the NIST WebBook; D₀ = {#} eV, Bishop {#}). The picture uses a compressed brightness scale so the tails show.",
"Energy levels (OpenStax §{#}): in the book's model the molecule vibrates as a harmonic oscillator, E_vib = (n + {#}/{#})hf, and turns as a rigid rotor, E_rot = l(l + {#})ħ²/2I with I = μr² and μ the reduced mass. Here hf is the measured spacing of n = {#} and n = {#} and ħ²/2I = B₀ (the measured rotational constant of n = {#}). A photon carries one unit of angular momentum, so l must change by {#} (Δl = ±{#}); in the harmonic model n changes by {#} or not at all, and only molecules with an electric dipole (HCl, HF, CO, not N₂) absorb. \"Measured constants\" uses the full term values: anharmonic vibration G(v) = ωe(v + {#}/{#}) − ωe xe(v + {#}/{#})² + ..., a rotational constant B_v that falls as v rises, and centrifugal stretching D_v [l(l + {#})]². Wavenumbers are in cm⁻¹; {#} cm⁻¹ is {#} meV.",
"HCl spectrum: the v = {#} → {#} band of HCl gas, simulated from the measured spectroscopic constants of H³⁵Cl (NIST WebBook, Huber and Herzberg {#}, with the higher terms of Rank, Rao et al. {#}). They reproduce the measured lines to about {#} cm⁻¹ (R({#}) {#}, P({#}) {#} cm⁻¹). H³⁷Cl constants are scaled from H³⁵Cl by the reduced mass (Born–Oppenheimer). Line strengths follow the Boltzmann population of the lower level, the Hönl–London factors and the isotope abundances ({#} % and {#} %); the slight difference in strength between the R and P branches from the stretching of the dipole (Herman–Wallis effect), pressure broadening and the weak hot band are left out. Each line is drawn as a Gaussian as wide as the spectrometer's resolution. Absorbance is relative to the strongest peak. The lock finds the top of the displayed peak, as you would by eye.",
"Molecules and light: a photon is absorbed only if the molecule has a way to take exactly its energy. Microwaves turn a molecule, and only one with a permanent electric dipole (CO, H₂O, NO₂, O₃; not N₂, O₂ or CO₂) feels the wave's field; a real absorber needs the photon on one of its rotational lines. Infrared sets a vibration going, and only one that changes the dipole: the bend of CO₂ at {#} cm⁻¹ ({#} μm) does, its symmetric stretch does not, and N₂ and O₂ have none that do; here the lamp counts as on a band within ±{#} cm⁻¹ of its center, about the reach of the rotational branches at room temperature. Visible and ultraviolet photons lift an electron, but only inside the molecule's absorption band; if the photon also carries more than the bond energy D₀, the molecule breaks (O₃ below {#} nm, NO₂ below {#} nm, O₂ below {#} nm), otherwise it glows. An excited molecule here gives back a photon of the color it took, in a random direction, about two seconds later on screen (really nanoseconds to milliseconds; in a gas, collisions often take the energy as heat first, and NO₂'s real glow is spread to longer wavelengths). Every allowed photon is taken, though real absorption strengths differ by large factors: ozone takes {#} nm light about two thousand times more readily than {#} nm. Vibration shapes are the textbook pictures, exaggerated; in a bent molecule the real symmetric stretch also bends slightly. Data: dipoles NIST CCCBDB; vibration frequencies Shimanouchi (NSRDS-NBS {#}) via the NIST WebBook; absorption band edges (approximate) and D₀ from Okabe, Photochemistry of Small Molecules ({#}), and the JPL evaluation {#}-{#} ({#}); geometry NIST CCCBDB.",
"Sources: OpenStax University Physics Vol. {#}, §{#} and §{#}; K. P. Huber and G. Herzberg, Constants of Diatomic Molecules ({#}), via the NIST Chemistry WebBook; atomic masses AME2020; chlorine abundances IUPAC (CIAAW); ionic radii R. D. Shannon, Acta Cryst. A32, {#} ({#}); H₂⁺: D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed. ({#}), §{#}, and P. Atkins and J. de Paula, Physical Chemistry, 9th ed. ({#}), §{#}; constants CODATA {#}.",
